# Build-in Methods for Iterables

| Method | Purpose | Syntax | Parameters | Return Value | Complexities |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| [**`list.sort()`**](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#list.sort) | Sort list **in-place**. **Only** defined for **lists**. Uses Timsort. | `list.sort(key=None, reverse=False)` | - **key** (Optional): A **function or callable** taking one argument for **sorting criteria**. **Default**: **lexicographical order** for nested lists (i.e., first sort by `x[0]`, if `x[0]` is the same, further sorts by `x[1]`). <br>  •  [*Multi-level of sorting*](https://docs.python.org/3/howto/sorting.html): <br>`from operator import itemgetter, attrgetter`<br>  `key = itemgetter(obj_idx_1, obj_idx_2)` or <br>  `key = attrgetter (obj_attr_1, obj_attr_2) ` <br>  •  [*use partial() to reduce arugments*](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functools.html#functools.partial):  <br> `from functools import partial`<br>  `key=partial(multi_argument_func)` <br> - **reverse** (Optional): : Boolean flag for order. **Default**: **ascending** (`False`); descending (`True`). | Returns `None` (modifies list in place) | Time: $O(n \log n)$<br>Space: $O(n)$ |
| [**`sorted()`**](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#sorted) | Build a **new sorted list** from **any iterable**. Uses Timsort. | `sorted(iterable, key=None, reverse=False)` | - **iterable**: **Any** iterable object.<br> - **key / reverse**: Same as `list.sort()`. | **New** sorted list | Time: $O(n \log n)$<br>Space: $O(n)$ |
| [**`zip()`**](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#zip) | **Combine multiple iterables** element-wise into **tuples**.<br><br>**Additional Tricks:**<br>• *Unzip*: <br> `var1, var2 = zip(*var)`<br>• *Chunking a list into equal size n*: <br> `zip(*[iter(s)] * n, strict=True)` | `zip(*iterables, strict=False)` | - ***iterables**: Two or more iterable inputs.<br> - **strict** (Optional): Boolean flag for equal length. <br> If `True`, forces equal length check. **Default** `False` (stops when the shortest iterable is exhausted, ignore the remaining items in the longer iterables). If iterables must to be of equal length, set strict=True | A iterator of tuples, one tuple per index position (a lazy iterator, so no actual tuples are created until you iterate or convert it using `list()`, `set()`, etc.) | Time: $O(n)$<br>Space: $O(1)$ (lazy, unless cast) |
